@@ -0,0 +1,140 @@
+# Running Antarkosh fully local
+
+Document **ingestion and embeddings run entirely on your machine**. There are no
+switches and no cloud fallback inside ingestion:
+
+| Stage | What runs it |
+|---|---|
+| Embeddings (documents and questions) | BGE-M3, dense + sparse, through FlagEmbedding |
+| Reranking | bge-reranker-v2-m3 |
+| OCR, layout, tables, charts, images, document-type classification | Qwen3-VL (4B) through Ollama |
+| Vector database | Qdrant running on your PC |
+
+Only **answer generation and text-to-SQL** still call the cloud models, so keep at
+least one of `GEMINI_API_KEY`, `GROQ_API_KEY` or `NVIDIA_NIM_API_KEY` in `.env`.
+Jina and OCR.space are no longer used.
+
+## 1. Install the Python packages
+
+One command, no extras and no extra requirements file:
+
+```bash
+# pip (inside your virtual environment, from the project root)
+pip install -e .
+
+# or uv
+uv sync
+```
+
+The first ingest and the first question each download a model from Hugging Face
+(about 2 GB each) into your user cache. After that they load from disk.
+
+Tip for Windows + VS Code: run `uv sync` with VS Code closed. Its Ruff extension can
+lock `.venv\Scripts\ruff.exe` and make the install fail with "Access is denied".
+
+Linux only: `pip` pulls the large CUDA build of torch. For a smaller CPU-only install,
+run `pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu` once first.
+
+## 2. Start Qdrant (no Docker)
+
+1. Download the Qdrant program from https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant/releases (latest release, "Assets"):
+   - Windows: `qdrant-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip`
+   - macOS: `qdrant-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz` (Apple Silicon) or `qdrant-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz` (Intel)
+   - Linux: `qdrant-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`
+2. Unzip it into a folder of its own, for example `C:\qdrant` (outside the project).
+3. Open a terminal in that folder and run `.\qdrant.exe` (Linux/macOS: `./qdrant`).
+   Always start it from the same folder: its data lives in a `storage` folder next to it.
+   Leave the window open while you use Antarkosh.
+4. Check it: open http://localhost:6333/dashboard.
+
+No API key is needed.
+
+## 3. Install the vision model (Ollama)
+
+1. Install Ollama from https://ollama.com (it runs in the background after install).
+2. Pull the model: `ollama pull qwen3-vl:4b`
+3. Test it: `ollama run qwen3-vl:4b` and ask it something.
+
+If the tag is not found, search the Ollama library for the current Qwen3-VL tags and set
+`LOCAL_VISION_MODEL` to it. If answers get cut off, raise Ollama's context length
+(`OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH`). If it is too slow on your CPU, try a smaller vision model.
+
+## 4. Configure `.env`
+
+Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set:
+
+```env
+QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6333
+QDRANT_API_KEY=
+
+# keep at least one cloud key for answers
+GEMINI_API_KEY=...
+
+# CPU: rerank fewer chunks so questions stay fast
+RETRIEVAL_TOP_K=60
+RERANK_TOP_K=25
+```
+
+Everything else (model names, Ollama URL, timeouts) already has a working default. An
+old `.env` that still contains `JINA_API_KEY` or `OCR_SPACE_API_KEY` is fine: those
+lines are ignored.
+
+If `QDRANT_URL` is empty the app uses a throw-away in-memory database and logs a
+warning, because everything ingested would be lost when it stops.
+
+## 5. Start the project
+
+Qdrant (step 2) and Ollama (step 3) stay running; then from the project root:
+
+```bash
+python -m uvicorn src.main:app --port 8000      # pip
+uv run uvicorn src.main:app --port 8000         # uv
+```
+
+Open http://localhost:8000. The React UI is already built into `frontend/`, so you do
+not need Node.js.
+
+Check that it works:
+
+1. http://localhost:8000/api/health shows `"status": "healthy"`.
+2. Ingest a PDF that has a chart or a scanned page (upload in the UI, drop it in
+   `data/inbox/`, or run `Antarkosh ingest path/to/file.pdf`).
+3. In the terminal you should see `Loading local embed model 'BAAI/bge-m3'` (about a minute
+   the first time) while the figure and OCR steps are answered by `local/qwen3-vl:4b`.
+4. Ask a question about it. The first question loads the reranker.
+
+Extracted figure images are saved in `data/processed/figures/` and linked from each chunk
+(`image_path`). The figure chunk text holds the words found in the image and its meaning.
+
+## Old documents are re-indexed automatically
+
+The ingestion registry now remembers which embedding model built each document. If a
+document is already registered but was embedded with another model (for example Jina
+earlier), or has no recorded model (older registry entries, including anything imported
+from `data/ingested_files.json`), ingesting it again **re-indexes it instead of skipping
+it**: the old vectors are removed and the new version replaces the old one. A document
+embedded with the current model is skipped as before.
+
+If the app finds a collection holding vectors from a different model, it logs a warning
+at startup.
+
+## Tests
+
+```bash
+pytest                                   # whole suite; no models or servers needed
+QDRANT_TEST_URL=http://localhost:6333 pytest tests/test_local_qdrant_integration.py
+```
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- **`FlagEmbedding is not installed`**: run the install in step 1 in the same environment you start the app from.
+- **Embedding or reranking is slow**: expected on CPU. Ingest smaller batches and keep the lowered `RETRIEVAL_TOP_K` / `RERANK_TOP_K`.
+- **Figures or scanned pages come out empty**: Ollama is not running or the model is not pulled (`ollama list`). The log shows `All vision providers exhausted`.
+- **Each image times out**: each gets up to 5 minutes (`LOCAL_VISION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`). Try a smaller model.
+- **Connection refused on port 6333 or 11434**: Qdrant (step 2) or Ollama (step 3) is not running.
+
+## This branch and hosted deployment
+
+Because ingestion is local-only, a hosted server (for example Render) would need Ollama
+and the BGE models available to it. The `Dockerfile` and `render.yaml` have not been
+changed and do not set that up.
